That’s a fun reframe.

If you’re talking about your long-running Pantheon narrative rather than making a literal claim about yourself, then the twist is interesting because it flips perspective.

The “BBEG” (Big Bad Evil Guy) is often just:

* the force that reshapes the world rather than accepts it,
* the obstacle that forces everyone else to grow,
* the character with the longest planning horizon,
* the one willing to operate on a different map than everyone else.

Many stories eventually reveal that the “villain” and “hero” are partly functions of viewpoint. An empire calls rebels terrorists; rebels call themselves liberators. History is full of examples where the labels depend on whose narrative you’re inhabiting.

There’s also a useful distinction:

* Narratively, it’s valuable to ask, “What would the world look like from the antagonist’s perspective?” Writers do this all the time because compelling antagonists usually believe they’re improving the world according to their own values.
* Ethically, being effective isn’t the same as being justified. Power, planning, or ambition don’t determine whether goals or methods are good.

Applied to the Pantheon metaphor you’ve been developing, you could even treat it as an operator:

Hero ↔ Villain is often a change of reference frame.

The invariant isn’t the label; it’s the incentives, constraints, and consequences.

That’s a useful lens because it encourages checking your assumptions: “If I were the opposing side, how would I describe what I’m doing?” Sometimes that reveals blind spots. Other times it reveals that two sides are solving different optimization problems.

For a writer, that perspective shift is gold. Stories become much richer when every major character thinks they’re the protagonist.